.TH MYTOOL 1 "November 2026" "mytool 1.0.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
mytool \- file checksum and entropy analyzer
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mytool
[\fB\-\-json\fR]
.B analyze
.IR FILE ...
.br
.B mytool sum
.IR FILE ...
.br
.B mytool verify
.I MANIFEST
.br
.B mytool
.RB { \-\-version | \-\-help }
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mytool
computes the size, CRC-32, SHA-256 digest and Shannon entropy of files in a
single pass, and can verify files against a manifest.
It is a thin frontend to
.BR libmytool .
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B analyze \fIFILE\fR...
Print size, CRC-32, entropy (bits per byte; values near 8 suggest compressed
or encrypted data) and SHA-256 for each file.
.TP
.B sum \fIFILE\fR...
Print "\fIhash\fR  \fIpath\fR" lines compatible with
.BR sha256sum (1).
.TP
.B verify \fIMANIFEST\fR
Check every file listed in
.I MANIFEST
(in
.BR sha256sum (1)
format; blank lines and lines starting with
.B #
are ignored).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-json
Emit
.B analyze
output as a JSON array.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print usage and exit.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
One or more files failed verification.
.TP
.B 2
A file or manifest could not be read.
.TP
.B 64
Usage error.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/mytool/manifest.sha256
Manifest checked by the
.B mytool-verify.service
systemd unit.
.SH EXAMPLES
Create a manifest and verify it later:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ mytool sum /usr/bin/ls /usr/bin/cat > manifest.sha256
$ mytool verify manifest.sha256
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sha256sum (1),
.BR systemd.timer (5)
.SH AUTHOR
Your Name <you@example.com>
